        "h": "The master grid — telling the red eyes apart",
        "body": "*✅ Tables 4-1 and 4-2 are IMAGES and extract EMPTY. They were **rendered on 2026-08-17** and are transcribed verbatim into the cache — so what follows is the book’s own grid, not a reading of its prose. ⚠️ An earlier reconstruction from the prose got two cells wrong and invented two columns; both are corrected here.*\n\n### Table 4-1 — SYMPTOMS, graded 0 (absent) to 3 (severe)\n⚠️ marks a row whose presence makes **referral advisable**.\n\n|Symptom|Acute glaucoma|Irido-cyclitis|Keratitis|Bacterial conj.|Viral conj.|Allergic conj.|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|⚠️ **Blurred vision**|**3**|1–2|**3**|0|0|0|\n|⚠️ **Pain**|2–3|2–3|2|0|0|0|\n|⚠️ **Photophobia**|**1**|**3**|**3**|0|0|0|\n|⚠️ **Coloured halos**|**2–3**|0|0|0|0|0|\n|**Exudation**|0|0|0–3|**3**|2|1|\n|**Itching**|0|0|0|0|0|**2–3**|\n\n- **⚠️ Photophobia is MILD in acute glaucoma:** graded **1** — the *lowest* of the three dangerous eyes, against **3** in both iridocyclitis and keratitis. It is the cell the prose reading got wrong.\n- **⚠️ Every conjunctivitis column scores 0** for blurred vision, pain and photophobia. That zero is the discriminator.\n- **Itching is the ALLERGIC marker:** 2–3 there, **0** in all five others.\n- **Exudation is GRADED, not yes/no:** bacterial **3** > viral **2** > allergic **1**.\n- **Coloured halos are unique to acute glaucoma** — 2–3 there, 0 everywhere else.\n\n### Table 4-2 — SIGNS\n`+` usually present · `−` usually absent · `+/−` may or may not be.\n\n|Sign|Acute glaucoma|Irido-cyclitis|Keratitis|Bacterial conj.|Viral conj.|Allergic conj.|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|⚠️ **Ciliary flush**|+|+|+|−|−|−|\n|**Conjunctival hyperaemia**|+|+|+|+|+|+|\n|⚠️ **Corneal opacification**|+|−|+|−|+/−|−|\n|⚠️ **Corneal epithelial disruption**|−|−|+|−|+/−|−|\n|⚠️ **Pupillary abnormalities**|+|+|+/−|−|−|−|\n|⚠️ **Shallow anterior chamber**|+|−|−|−|−|−|\n|⚠️ **Elevated IOP**|+|+/−|−|−|−|−|\n|⚠️ **Proptosis**|−|−|−|−|−|−|\n|**Discharge**|−|−|+/−|+|+|+|\n|**Preauricular node**|−|−|−|−|+|−|\n\n- **⚠️ Discharge:** ABSENT in acute glaucoma and iridocyclitis — printed `−` for both. The prose reading called them \"watery\"; the table does not.\n- **⚠️ Conjunctival hyperaemia:** `+` in ALL SIX, and the only \"No\" in the referral column among the signs — **redness alone decides nothing.**\n- **Preauricular node is VIRAL only.** Shallow chamber is **acute glaucoma alone**.\n- **⚠️ Proptosis is `−` in all six:** it is on the grid to be **excluded** — a proptosed red eye is not on this list at all.\n\n### ⚠️ The two findings that decide everything\n- **REDUCED VISION and a CORNEAL OPACITY.** Neither is produced by conjunctival or episcleral disease.\n- **The three referral-worthy symptoms:** blurred vision, pain and photophobia — and all three are **0** in every conjunctivitis.\n\n### The referral rule\n- **⚠️ One or more danger signals:** the patient has a disorder **requiring an ophthalmologist’s attention**.\n- **⚠️ Reduced vision:** any red eye with it **is referred, never treated blind**.\n- **⚠️ Soft contact lenses:** any red eye in a wearer **is referred**.\n\n*⚠️ Divergence, recorded: Table 4-2 prints elevated IOP as `+/−` in iridocyclitis — it **may** be raised. The commoner teaching is that it runs **low** from ciliary shutdown. Both stand; `L37`’s own table is this chapter’s source.*\n\n*The fixed, mid-dilated **5–6 mm** pupil of acute angle closure is `L37`’s prose, not its table; `op-glauc` (`glc-14`) writes the attack in full.*\n\n*`op-cornea` (`cor-9`) carries a four-column grid from the same source — keratitis, conjunctivitis, episcleritis, dry eye. This is the book’s own six-column version, and it adds the bacterial/viral/allergic split that `cor-9` has no room for.*\n\nSrc: L37 Tables 4-1 and 4-2, p.19 (rendered 2026-08-17); lines 312–330, 331–479, 705–706; L1,2 lines 629–641 (cited only, not counted)",
